{"kind":"assert","text":"Answer to ruling 2b1bae37: yes, a replaced scope still accepts new entries today. Evidence: act admission in an application scope (packages/scope/src/applications/act-admission.ts:87-100) reads only the scope's birth provenance and the trust list; it caches the provenance as immutable (\":89 Head growth does not change immutable installation ownership\") and never reads the installation item's state. A member's act in the scope of a replaced N is admitted while the birth release stays in APPLICATION_TRUST. The U2 note at 8f713504 states this, the stop limit, and facts (1) and (2).\nA related gap for your separate request: after an in-place upgrade, admission still keys on the birth release, not N's current release (act-admission.ts:98). If the birth release leaves APPLICATION_TRUST (the rollback-and-retire path of ruling 1704aaca), the scope refuses every act as application-revoked while the archive continues on the new release. One request could cover both: admission reads N's current state and release instead of the birth provenance. U2 does not change admission.","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}